A||||16||walsh2001||Clutch Problem.... Help!!!||||11||
Z||000000||walsh2001||04-12-2001||09:09 AM||||When I depress the clutch, the pressure plate and clutch do not completely separate. This makes it impossible to shift while the engine is running. At first, I thought it was a hydraulic problem and rebuilt the slave and master cyclinders; however, it is still doing it. I watched the slave cylinder while a friend depressed the clutch, and it seems to be moving the lever (clutch fork) fine. Any ideas? Could this be a simple adjustment?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000001||walsh2001||04-12-2001||09:14 AM||||My Fiero is an 84 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||04-12-2001||09:46 AM||||Check Ed Park's Fiero Factory page, it has some clutch troubleshooting ideas on it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||86GT Turbo||04-12-2001||10:31 AM||||You might have a broken clutch fork.<BR>but I would try bleeding it for a while<BR>A friend of mine who worked at pontiac<BR>assy said the way they use to do it on the line is depress your pedle to the floor<BR>and then move your foot to the side and<BR>let the pedle snap up on its own<BR>I find thats the way it works best for me.<BR>? can you shift the car when its not <BR>running.<P>hope this helps||||reg||5
Z||000004||86GT Turbo||04-12-2001||10:31 AM||||You might have a broken clutch fork.<BR>but I would try bleeding it for a while<BR>A friend of mine who worked at pontiac<BR>assy said the way they use to do it on the line is depress your pedle to the floor<BR>and then move your foot to the side and<BR>let the pedle snap up on its own<BR>I find thats the way it works best for me.<BR>? can you shift the car when its not <BR>running.<P>hope this helps||||reg||5
Z||000005||walsh2001||04-12-2001||10:38 AM||||Yes I can shift fine when the car is not running||||reg||1
Z||000006||86GT Turbo||04-12-2001||10:51 AM||||if you can shift when the can is not running<BR>then you have air in your line<BR>try jacking up the rear wheels about <BR>18 in and then try to bleed it<BR>if you have time leave it sit for a while<BR>with the bleed screw loose and let <BR>gravity take its course<BR>the last thing it might be is a leak in your<BR>line but I'v only ever seen that once<BR>and that was on a VERY rusted out fiero<P><BR>Turbo||||reg||14
Z||000007||walsh2001||04-12-2001||10:53 AM||||I did bleed it (not with the wheels jacked) and the slave cyclinder seems to be working.(It moves about 2 inched).||||reg||1
Z||000008||86GT Turbo||04-12-2001||11:00 AM||||<P>try the mount point for the slave<BR>on the trans if one of those brackets are bent that might be it.<BR>if not you might have a enternal clutch<BR>problem.<P><BR>Turbo<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000009||walsh2001||04-12-2001||11:04 AM||||:O<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||walsh2001||04-12-2001||11:08 AM||||When replacing (repairing) a fork, do I have to drop the engine? If so, do I have to lower it all the way or just enough to access the clutch? Is the repair fairly easy beyond that?<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000011||86GT Turbo||04-12-2001||11:12 AM||||Sorry <P>you have to take the trans all the way out<BR>you need a special puller to get the<BR>fork bushing out<BR>you have to put the car up at least 2ft<BR>in the back support the motor on top with a chain and drop the cradle bolts in the back<BR>you could then pull it out the bottom<BR>its a real pain in the a**<BR>but its better then paying a shop $900<BR>bucks to do it.<P>Turbo||||reg||1
Z||000012||Steve Normington||04-12-2001||11:17 AM||||I had this same problem. Check the clutch lever itself (the lever on the outside that the slave cylinder pushes on) for cracks around the clutch fork rod. If it is cracked, you can replace it with a cast steel instead of the OE stamped.<P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000013||walsh2001||04-12-2001||11:22 AM||||Thanks, i will look into it. I really do not want to drop an engine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||walsh2001||04-12-2001||11:24 AM||||Also... When I pump the pedal before starting the car it works fine (sometimes). This is what prompted me to rebuild the slave and master cylinders. Unfortunately, it is still doing it.||||reg||4
Z||000015||Steve Normington||04-12-2001||03:12 PM||||Before it quit completely, did it get harder to shift? Before mine went, I had to jam the pedal clear to the floor or I'd crunch the gears.<P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000016||wla21841||04-12-2001||03:46 PM||||I had that problem a long time ago. 1 as stated before the stamped clutch lever does not hold up, I replaced it with a cast one. The master cylander had a bad check valve in it. Bleed the hell out of it. By the sound of your last post, you still have air in the system. If you can pump it up and i works then there is air in the system.||||reg||1
